      "updated": "2026-11-20T07:28:43+00:00",
      "page": 8,
      "summary": "Proposition 1 identifies the first change in the support of the unique optimal reproduction law. Positive persistence loses both transition words together. Negative persistence loses the rarer constant, or both constants at equal bias. At zero persistence all four words remain active below the zero-rate distortion.",
      "sketch": "The two transition words have the same candidate mass. Substitute u = D/(1−D) in their common numerator and use the fact that the four input probabilities sum to one. Multiplication by the positive factor (1−D)² gives C−D(1−D), so both numerators vanish when D(1−D) = C. For the constant word 00, multiplying its numerator by B gives the square (Bu-C)^2 plus AB-C^2. The latter is strictly positive by the pair-mass identity. Exchanging A and B covers 11. The smaller quadratic root lies strictly between zero and one half. Factoring the scaled transition mass shows its sign agrees with the threshold minus the distortion on that interval. At zero persistence set C = pq, A = p - C and B = q - C. The four inverse-prior masses simplify to the products of the one-symbol factors (p-D)/(1-2D) and (q-D)/(1-2D). Both factors are positive below min(p,q) and sum to one.",
      "step": "Proposition 1 is closed. Lean checked the complete stationary Markov statement, all four support probabilities, uniqueness of the minimizing marginal and every endpoint claimed in the proposition.",
      "next": "Paper-wide work continues with Theorem 3.",
      "run_log": "evidence/persistent-pair-complete.txt",
      "scope": "For every stationary two-state source with positive transition probabilities, the contract compares mutual information against every competing joint law with the same input and distortion budget. It proves existence and uniqueness of the optimal reproduction marginal and gives its exact support before and at each first threshold, including the unbiased tie, zero persistence and lossless distortion. Mutual information is in nats, which preserves the minimizer under conversion to bits.",
      "structured": "Source: local manuscript, Proposition 1, equation labeled eq:pairprior and the following sentence. The numbered steps below expand its algebra; they are a pilot supplement.\n⟨1⟩1. Set u = D/(1−D) and let T = C(1+u²)−(A+B)u.\n⟨1⟩2. Multiply T by (1−D)².\n  ⟨2⟩1. Use A+B = 1−2C from pair-mass normalization.\n  ⟨2⟩2. Expand and collect terms to obtain C−D(1−D).\n⟨1⟩3. Since D < 1/2, the factor in step 2 is nonzero. Thus T = 0 exactly when D(1−D) = C.\n⟨1⟩4. For 00, B times its numerator is (Bu-C)^2 + AB-C^2. The pair-mass identity gives AB-C^2 > 0 under positive persistence. Since B > 0, the numerator is positive. Exchange A and B to obtain the same conclusion for 11.\n⟨1⟩5. The smaller root r lies in (0, 1/2) and r(1-r)=C. The sign of the transition mass equals the sign of (r-D)(1-r-D). For D<1/2 the second factor is strictly positive, so the smaller root is the first zero.\n⟨1⟩6. For the independent case use the two factors from the sketch. Substitute C = pq into each candidate mass, use p+q = 1 and clear denominators. The two factors are positive because D < min(p,q) implies p-D, q-D and 1-2D are positive.\nNegative persistence: the discriminant follows from stationary flow. Independently, the quadratic root and the constant-word comparison give the candidate zero and tie. The root bounds supply the slope hypothesis needed for positivity of both the rarer constant and the transition words. The ordered Markov lemma combines these facts. Exchanging the labels and passing from u to D remain.",
